th='A humorous story of a vain young doctor whose pride melts when a snake coils around his arm; a legend about greed punished (poem)',
sum='A young homoeopath (the narrator) lives in a small rented room with a tiled roof; his earnings are meagre (he has about sixty rupees). Rats run to and from the beam above – the “traffic of rats”. One hot night he returns, looks into the mirror and admires himself – he decides to shave daily, grow a thin moustache, always smile, and marry a rich, fat lady doctor with plenty of money (fat, so that if he made a silly mistake and ran away, she could not run after him and catch him!). Suddenly a fat snake drops from the beam onto his shoulder and coils around his left arm. He is turned to stone with fear, holds his breath, and prays to God in his imagination. Then the snake uncoils and slides to the table, towards the mirror – perhaps to admire its own reflection. The doctor jumps out of the window and runs away. The next morning a thief has stolen his things, leaving only a dirty vest. The story is a humorous tale about human vanity and how a crisis brings a person back to reality. Poem “A Legend of the Northland” (Phoebe Cary): in the cold Northland, Saint Peter, hungry and weak, asks a little old lady for a cake from her store; she bakes smaller and smaller cakes but finds each too big to give away, and gives him nothing. Angered, he turns her into a woodpecker who must bore all day in the hard, dry wood for her scanty food – a lesson against greed and selfishness.',
